\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Bolometric luminosity from the Type~Ia (left) and kilonova (right) validation simulations. For each model we compare the single-group calculation using $\kappa _R^{\rm  exp}$ (solid, circles) with the corresponding full frequency-group calculation (dashed, squares). The kilonova runs share the same ejecta profile and heating rate and differ only in the lanthanide mass fraction of the composition, and hence in the opacity table. The final observer times, which can receive photons emitted after the simulation ends, are excluded by requiring $t<t_{\rm  end}(1-\beta _{\rm  max})$.}}{8}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:comparison}{{7}{8}{Bolometric luminosity from the Type~Ia (left) and kilonova (right) validation simulations. For each model we compare the single-group calculation using $\kappa _R^{\rm exp}$ (solid, circles) with the corresponding full frequency-group calculation (dashed, squares). The kilonova runs share the same ejecta profile and heating rate and differ only in the lanthanide mass fraction of the composition, and hence in the opacity table. The final observer times, which can receive photons emitted after the simulation ends, are excluded by requiring $t<t_{\rm end}(1-\beta _{\rm max})$}{figure.7}{}}
